 <section class="sim-panel" aria-labelledby="sims">
  <h2 id="sims">In the sim</h2>
  <p><strong>DCS F-16C.</strong> The guide’s sequence matches the real one: call the tanker, join from behind and below with the closure falling off, then the pre-refuelling checklist: master arm and laser off, emitters to standby before the boom, AIR REFUEL switch open 3–5 minutes early if you carry tanks. In what the guide calls pre-contact, the ATP’s astern, you match the tanker’s speed; cleared, move forward with small, slow throttle changes, keeping lined up on the KC-135’s yellow belly stripe. <a href="#source-dcs">{dcs('160–163')}</a> Its advice in contact is to fly formation on the tanker itself, using the centre fuselage at the wing roots as the reference, and not to stare at the boom or the lights. Breakaway is forward stick to descend and throttle back. <a href="#source-dcs">{dcs('164–165')}</a></p>
  <p><strong>Falcon BMS.</strong> Training mission 4D has KC-10, KC-135 and KC-130 scenarios, and since 4.36 probe-and-drogue refuelling with the F/A-18C and other probe receivers on the KC-135, KC/DC-10, KC-130H, C-160 and Il-78M. <a href="#source-bms">{bms('118, 128')}</a> It gives a closure rule of 10 kt per 1,000 ft, never more than 10 kt inside 1,000 ft, and says to fly the boom like fingertip formation, on the tanker rather than the HUD. <a href="#source-bms">{bms('120–122')}</a> Its pod lights match the NATO standard: red not available, amber ready, green fuel flowing; push too far and the basket disconnects. Its basket advice is a constant approach speed and, again, don’t chase the basket. <a href="#source-bms">{bms('128–129')}</a></p>
  <p><strong>Where the sims and the ATP differ.</strong></p>
  <ul>
   <li>Both sims call the boom astern position pre-contact. BMS places it 50 ft below at 30° down from the tanker; the ATP says about 50 ft behind and slightly below the nozzle. <a href="#source-bms">{bms('121')}</a> <a href="#source-atp">{atp('Annex 1A')}</a></li>
   <li>The director-light colours are described differently: the DCS guide says green is a slight correction and red the boom’s limits, BMS that red is furthest, yellow close and green ideal. The ATP leaves light details to each nation’s documents, so this module claims neither. <a href="#source-dcs">{dcs('163')}</a> <a href="#source-bms">{bms('123')}</a> <a href="#source-atp">{atp('¶1.15.a')}</a></li>
   <li>When the director lights come on: the ATP says the boom’s sensors turn them on once the aircraft are in contact, while both sims light them from the clearance to contact so you can fly them into position. Figure 2 follows the sims; with the ATP’s lights, the boom operator’s talk-on guides the approach. <a href="#source-atp">{atp('¶1.15.a, ¶2.8.f(4), ¶2.46')}</a> <a href="#source-dcs">{dcs('163')}</a> <a href="#source-bms">{bms('121')}</a></li>
   <li>The BMS tanker banks 30° in its racetrack turns; the ATP plans rendezvous turns at 25°. <a href="#source-bms">{bms('123')}</a> <a href="#source-atp">{atp('¶2.2.f')}</a></li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Not checked.</strong> The DCS F/A-18C guide has no refuelling section, so DCS probe-and-drogue refuelling, the DCS AI tankers’ radio calls and flow, and other DCS modules were not checked. Neither sim manual describes Echelon Left waiting, the breakaway routes for each hose or silent signals, so nothing is claimed about them.</p>
  <p><strong>To practise:</strong> fly the opening example’s calls with a friend as tanker on a separate frequency, join Echelon Left and stabilize before every move, and practise breakaways on the call at a safe height.</p>
 </section>

