 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <p>One or more questions for each outcome at the top of the page; Figure 4 covers outcome 2, Figure 1 outcome 3, Figures 2 and 3 outcome 4, and the two quizzes outcome 5.</p>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> A probe-equipped fighter needs fuel and the only tanker is a boom tanker. What has to have happened on the ground?</summary><p>A boom drogue adapter has to have been fitted: it can only be fitted or removed on the ground. With it, the director lights are not used (only their elevation background lights) and there is no hose response, so close carefully.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> Who makes the contact on a boom, and who on a drogue?</summary><p>On a boom the boom operator flies the boom into the receptacle while the receiver holds formation; on a drogue the receiver pilot flies the probe into the drogue.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> You are 2 NM from the tanker in haze, not visual, with no radar. How close may you come and at what height?</summary><p>Keep at least 1,000 ft below and do not go inside 1 NM without the tanker in sight. Hold 1,000 ft below at 1 NM and wait for the tanker to fly into better visibility, or end the rendezvous.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> In a point-parallel rendezvous you realise you will pass the tanker before it rolls out. What do you do?</summary><p>Make sure you pass at least 1,000 ft below, call “(tanker) execute overrun”, slow down on the refuelling heading. Don’t climb until it is clear the tanker will stay ahead for the whole closure.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> Why join Echelon Left rather than going straight behind a drogue tanker?</summary><p>Distance and closure from behind are the hardest to judge, especially at night; on the wing, errors in line and overtake can be corrected clear of the tanker.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> You have finished and are cleared to leave. Can you descend away?</summary><p>Not without coordination: leave level or climbing, from Echelon Right, because other receivers are likely to be joining from below. Heavy boom receivers are different: the tanker climbs and the receiver descends within the block.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> In contact on a drogue the light goes green. What happened, and how far can you push?</summary><p>You have pushed the hose in far enough, typically about 2 m, to open the fuel valve. Fuel flows over a range of typically about 7 m further; on some equipment pushing beyond it closes the valve.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> Astern a boom you are still closing slowly when the boom operator clears you to contact. What should you have done first?</summary><p>Stabilize with zero closure. Most damaged booms come from receivers closing too fast and passing the inner limit; if you can’t stabilize, expect a breakaway.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> On the right wing hose you hear “Shell 6, breakaway, breakaway, breakaway.” Where do you go?</summary><p>Disconnect immediately, move back and out to the right wing, clear of the area behind the tanker. Getting clear is your responsibility.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> Radio silent, behind a boom tanker, the director lights go out while you are in contact. What does the tanker want?</summary><p>A disconnect and a return to the astern position.</p></details>

 <h2 id="sources">Sources and applicability</h2>
 <h3>Military source</h3>
 <p id="source-atp"><strong>[1] NATO Standardization Agency, ATP-3.3.4.2 (ATP-56), Air-to-Air Refuelling, Edition C Version 1, November 2013.</strong> <a href="https://bloximages.chicago2.vip.townnews.com/militarynews.com/content/tncms/assets/v3/editorial/5/ca/5ca69442-c74c-11e8-97a8-fff1004e63f5/5bb528e613e83.pdf.pdf">Copy hosted by militarynews.com</a>, read 3 November 2026: Chapter 1 Section III, Chapter 2 Sections I, IV and V with Annexes 2A, 2D, 2O, 2P and 2Q, Chapter 3 Sections I and II, and the lexicon. Cited by paragraph or annex, because this edition repeats page numbers between chapters. The publication NATO nations agreed under STANAG 3971; its letter of promulgation reserves reproduction, so this page summarizes and cites it. Later editions may exist and could not be read.</p>
 <h3>Sim sources</h3>
 <p id="source-dcs"><strong>[2] Eagle Dynamics, DCS: F-16C Viper Early Access Guide (English), updated 16 August 2026.</strong> <a href="https://www.digitalcombatsimulator.com/en/downloads/documentation/">DCS documentation page</a>, read 3 November 2026, pp. 160–165. A sim manual, cited only for the In the sim panel.</p>
 <p id="source-bms"><strong>[3] Benchmark Sims, Falcon BMS 4.38.1 Training Manual.</strong> <a href="https://cdn.falcon-bms.com/docs/4.38/BMS-Training-Manual.pdf">falcon-bms.com</a>, read 3 November 2026, pp. 118–129 (mission 4D). A sim manual, cited only for the In the sim panel.</p>
 <p>Page-specific excerpts are recorded in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/aar-evidence.md">air-to-air refuelling evidence</a>.</p>
 <p>Figures 1 to 4 and the opening example are original drawings and examples, not reproductions of the ATP’s figures. The receivers in Figures 2 and 3 are outlines traced from public-domain photographs: an F-16 refuelling from a KC-10 (US Air Force, <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KC-10_Extender_(2151957820).jpg">Wikimedia Commons</a>) and NASA’s F/A-18 No. 847 (NASA/Lori Losey, 2002, <a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/EC02-0294-1">EC02-0294-1</a>), drawn at their real lengths to each figure’s scale. Positions in Figure 1 show the order of the flow, not distances; the boom envelope in Figure 2 is invented; Figure 4 is calculated, not taken from the ATP’s tables.</p>
 <p class="note">Applicability: written for tanker and receiver crews of every service and nation. The procedures are NATO’s standard multinational ones; national tanker documents, which the ATP calls national annexes or SRDs, add each tanker’s limits, light signals and differences and were not read. US service publications for refuelling could not be read for this revision. Your service’s rules, the tanker’s national documents and the aircraft’s flight manual govern.</p>

